course: dictionary
lesson: 12579
title: A noisy word: din
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A din is a loud, long, unpleasant noise, often lots of sounds mixed together. «The playground was a din of shouts, whistles and bouncing balls.» The clues are shouts, whistles and bouncing balls, so din must mean a lot of loud noise.
explain: Look at the words around it. «She had to shout above the din to reach her friend.» Above the din means over the loud noise, so the room must be very noisy. Always ask: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Din can also be a verb, but it is rare. To din something into someone means to make them learn it by saying it again and again. «My teacher dinned the spelling rules into us all year.» The sentence shows you which meaning is used, so check the words around it.
fact: A din is a loud, long, unpleasant noise, often from many sounds together.
fact: «Make a din» means to make a loud noise.
fact: «Above the din» means over the loud noise, so people have to shout to be heard.
fact: «What a din!» is a way to say that a place is very noisy.
fact: «To din something into someone» means to make them learn it by repeating it again and again. This use of din is rare.
exercise: «The building site made a terrible din all morning.» What is a din here? | A soft, gentle breeze | A large, empty room | A tasty meal | *A loud, unpleasant noise
exercise: «She had to shout above the din to reach her friend.» What does above the din tell you? | She was singing a song | *The room was very noisy | The room was empty and silent | Her friend was sleeping
exercise: Mr Patel says, «Please stop this din!» Which word in the sentence means the noise? | stop | this | please | *din
test: «The teacher dinned the times tables into us every week.» In this sentence, dinned means | made us tired and hungry | made a loud crash with drums | *made us learn them by repeating them again and again | made us laugh until we cried
test: Which sentence uses din to mean a loud noise? | «He dinned the facts into his brother.» | «The dinner is ready, so come and eat.» | *«I could not hear her over the din.»
exam: Which meaning fits din in «make a din»? | To tidy up the room | A dinner plate | *A loud, long noise | Quiet thinking time
